{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "article": {
    "id": "re-foundations-8b-do-animals-laugh",
    "title": "RE Foundations / 8B / DO ANIMALS LAUGH?",
    "text_type": "informational",
    "audience": {
      "grade_band": "upper_elementary"
    },
    "language": "en"
  },
  "content": {
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "order": 1,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s1",
            "text": "We know animals have emotions."
          },
          {
            "id": "s2",
            "text": "They can feel fear."
          },
          {
            "id": "s3",
            "text": "We also think they feel love, since they have strong relationships with each other."
          },
          {
            "id": "s4",
            "text": "So are animal emotions similar to our own?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s5",
            "text": "And do animals have a sense of humor?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "order": 2,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s7",
            "text": "In fact, Bongo Marie clearly didn’t like Paco at all!"
          },
          {
            "id": "s8",
            "text": "One day, Blanchard cooked a chicken for dinner."
          },
          {
            "id": "s9",
            "text": "She started to cut the chicken with a knife."
          },
          {
            "id": "s10",
            "text": "“Oh, no! Paco!” Bongo Marie said loudly and laughed."
          },
          {
            "id": "s11",
            "text": "Blanchard laughed, too, and said, “That’s not Paco.”"
          },
          {
            "id": "s12",
            "text": "“Oh … no,” said Bongo Marie."
          },
          {
            "id": "s13",
            "text": "This time, she sounded disappointed."
          },
          {
            "id": "s14",
            "text": "Then the parrot laughed at her own joke."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "order": 3,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s21",
            "text": "Jean Donaldson enjoys yoga—and so does her dog Buffy."
          },
          {
            "id": "s22",
            "text": "While Donaldson does yoga, Buffy carefully places her toys on Donaldson’s body."
          },
          {
            "id": "s23",
            "text": "If a toy falls, Buffy runs to put it back."
          },
          {
            "id": "s24",
            "text": "Does this behavior have any real purpose?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s28",
            "text": "Does Buffy enjoy yoga?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "order": 4,
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "s30",
            "text": "Can dogs “laugh”?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s31",
            "text": "Recent research shows that dogs can tell each other when they want to play."
          },
          {
            "id": "s32",
            "text": "They make a special sound—a kind of “laugh.”"
          },
          {
            "id": "s33",
            "text": "Psychologist Patricia Simonet recorded the sound."
          },
          {
            "id": "s34",
            "text": "Then she played it back to dogs and studied their behavior."
          },
          {
            "id": "s35",
            "text": "“All the dogs seemed to like the laugh,” says Simonet."
          },
          {
            "id": "s36",
            "text": "So do animals have a sense of humor?"
          },
          {
            "id": "s37",
            "text": "If laughter is a clue, then perhaps the answer is “yes!”"
          },
          {
            "id": "s42",
            "text": "How do the dogs tell each other when they want to play?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
